  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>The Mills Hotel is in the directories from 1869 to 1915, on the corner of <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref> and <ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref>, and it is listed under both. From 1900 it is No. 259 Queensberry Street. From 1905 the directories call the corner the intersection of Barry and Queensberry streets and Leicester Street.</p>
        <p>The directories list the proprietors as Patrick J. Langan (1869 to 1871), Martin Hughes (1872 and 1873), T. McGrath (1874), John Crowl (1875, also printed Crawl), John H. Barber (1876 to 1878), William Dartnell (1879 to 1895), Mrs Emily Chapman (1900), Mrs Bessie Locke (1905), Jas. Hannabry (1910) and A. McPhee (1915, Miss in the Leicester Street listing and Mrs in the Queensberry Street one).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>In the Leicester Street listings the hotel's neighbours are R. Halstead, dealer (1869 to 1871), John Holding, wood and coal dealer (1872), and William Goddard, furniture dealer, at No. 23 (1873 to 1877). In Queensberry Street T. Huxley, tailor, is at No. 14 in 1872 and 1873, and John Warne, butcher, in 1879. <ref target="civic?id=Dight%27s%20Flour%20Mills">Dight's Flour Mills</ref> and the <ref target="civic?id=Carlton%20Flour%20Mills">Carlton Flour Mills</ref> are listed just past the Queensberry Street corner in Leicester Street, up to 1880. <ref target="civic?id=Royal%20and%20Co">Royal and Co.</ref>, engineers, are also listed by this corner.</p>
      </div>
